WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.964 --> 00:00:14.812
My ship sails in the morning from the port of Lagos, Portugal.

2
00:00:16.073 --> 00:00:21.897
I'm supervising my sailors as they lower crates into the cargo area, check the sails for tears and

3
00:00:21.957 --> 00:00:23.518
holes, and test the ropes.

4
00:00:25.379 --> 00:00:31.583
India is our destination, by order of Prince Henry of Portugal, and we will be exploring seas we've

5
00:00:31.663 --> 00:00:32.704
never seen before.

6
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.242
My name is Edmundo Veloso.

7
00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:42.888
The year is 1434, and I have just been promoted to captain of the Terra Nova, a ship

8
00:00:42.988 --> 00:00:44.249
both fast and true.

9
00:00:45.871 --> 00:00:51.536
I am full of curiosity and armed with tools that have served mariners for years and years and

10
00:00:51.556 --> 00:00:52.337
the latest charts.

11
00:00:53.538 --> 00:00:55.840
This is the opportunity I've been working for.

12
00:00:57.416 --> 00:01:02.438
I first set foot on a ship at age 12 when, without warning, my father took me to

13
00:01:02.458 --> 00:01:05.098
the docks and set me up as an apprentice on a ship.

14
00:01:06.219 --> 00:01:09.940
I came from a big family, and my father could not afford to feed everyone.

15
00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.322
So over the next twenty years, I learned everything about sailing.

16
00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:18.603
I swabbed the decks and polished the brass.

17
00:01:19.943 --> 00:01:22.984
I learned how to rig the sails, navigate, and steer the ship.

18
00:01:24.088 --> 00:01:28.129
Climbing the rigging like a monkey to set the sails was more than a job for me; it

19
00:01:28.169 --> 00:01:29.790
was the most fun I ever had.

20
00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:35.111
The crow's nest, high atop the highest mast, was my favorite place.

21
00:01:36.491 --> 00:01:41.293
Although I was not enchanted with the sea as a child, I became enchanted as I grew up

22
00:01:41.413 --> 00:01:41.593
on it.

23
00:01:43.753 --> 00:01:47.294
The reign of Prince Henry of Portugal is an exciting time to be a mariner.

24
00:01:48.595 --> 00:01:53.096
Henry's goal is to find a sea route to India by heading south along the west coast of

25
00:01:53.216 --> 00:02:00.348
Africa. A man of science, Prince Henry wants to find a faster route to trade for spices and

26
00:02:00.408 --> 00:02:05.993
precious metals from the East, to spread Christianity, and perhaps even to discover new fishing grounds.

27
00:02:07.815 --> 00:02:12.199
The port of Lagos is close to Prince Henry's villa, where I had dinner a few nights ago

28
00:02:12.259 --> 00:02:16.723
and met cartographers, astrologers, navigators, and other explorers.

29
00:02:18.284 --> 00:02:25.797
It was an inspiring evening, and I came to understand Henry's plan— to use scientific methods in exploration

30
00:02:26.158 --> 00:02:29.479
and publish our findings for the benefit of those who come after us.

31
00:02:31.381 --> 00:02:36.964
Among the tools I use to navigate are the nocturnal, the astrolabe, and the mariner's compass.

32
00:02:38.645 --> 00:02:41.306
The nocturnal allows me to determine the time at night.

33
00:02:41.926 --> 00:02:45.628
I locate the Big Dipper, stars in an easy-to-see constellation.

34
00:02:46.629 --> 00:02:51.912
Two of its stars line up and point to the North Star— that's why they're called pointer stars.

35
00:02:53.204 --> 00:02:56.625
I line up the hole in the center of my nocturnal with the North Star.

36
00:02:58.045 --> 00:03:02.947
Then I swing the long arm of the nocturnal until it lines up with the Big Dipper's pointer

37
00:03:02.987 --> 00:03:04.287
stars and read the time!

38
00:03:05.607 --> 00:03:11.089
With the astrolabe I find my position by measuring how high the noon sun is over the horizon.

39
00:03:12.469 --> 00:03:18.251
From that measurement I can determine latitude—how far north or south our ship is from the equator.

40
00:03:19.511 --> 00:03:26.173
My mariner's compass has a magnetized needle that points north, which also helps me determine my position—along with

41
00:03:26.213 --> 00:03:27.154
my sea charts of course.

42
00:03:28.574 --> 00:03:32.716
These charts have lines that have been drawn to show the direction of the winds, as well as

43
00:03:32.756 --> 00:03:36.838
descriptions of landmarks, which I also use to figure out where I am.

44
00:03:38.079 --> 00:03:41.841
I keep a record of the time I sail on each bearing (direction) on the compass.

45
00:03:43.081 --> 00:03:49.004
With this method, my tools, and my years of experience, I navigate with certainty on the open seas.

46
00:03:50.548 --> 00:03:54.892
Seven years ago I was the first mate on the ship that discovered the Azores Islands.

47
00:03:56.273 --> 00:04:01.497
Having no land in sight was worrisome since we couldn't rely on our sea charts, but the results

48
00:04:01.717 --> 00:04:04.259
were well worth it—discovering a new land!

49
00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.703
Tomorrow we will sail south along the African coast.

50
00:04:09.684 --> 00:04:15.728
My ship, a caravel, carries different types of sails, which will allow us to maneuver easily and quickly

51
00:04:15.848 --> 00:04:16.289
in the wind.

52
00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:23.038
Long, thin, and shallow, the caravel is exceptional for exploring the coasts.

53
00:04:24.339 --> 00:04:27.801
It has taken time to assemble my crew of twenty, but they are ready.

54
00:04:28.822 --> 00:04:33.305
Many of them are young men who remind me of myself at that age—full of excitement.

55
00:04:34.446 --> 00:04:39.710
These young sailors are apprehensive, but their curiosity and sense of adventure overcome their fear.

56
00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:45.353
The older men are experienced, and because of this, they know the sea is unforgiving.

57
00:04:46.915 --> 00:04:48.856
They realize the weather can change in a moment.

58
00:04:49.808 --> 00:04:52.409
One morning the sky can be blue and the sea calm.

59
00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:58.372
But by noon a storm might arise, creating giant waves that can capsize any ship.

60
00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.454
All my men are superstitious and fearful at times.

61
00:05:04.675 --> 00:05:10.618
For instance, some believe the farther south one sails, the hotter the water becomes, because the sea gets

62
00:05:10.698 --> 00:05:12.378
colder as one sails northward.

63
00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:15.760
They fear southern seas will boil them.

64
00:05:17.428 --> 00:05:22.433
Others believe we will reach the edge of the earth and fall off, or that monsters lurk in

65
00:05:22.453 --> 00:05:25.196
the deep, just waiting for an unsuspecting ship.

66
00:05:26.277 --> 00:05:29.940
Many who have families are not willing to take the risk of a long sea voyage.

67
00:05:31.161 --> 00:05:35.726
I respect their fears because, in fact, I am not immune to them now that I have a

68
00:05:35.786 --> 00:05:36.647
family of my own.

69
00:05:38.388 --> 00:05:41.832
But to me this trip is the climax of my career.

70
00:05:43.688 --> 00:05:48.171
I tell the crew of my pride in being on the first ship to sight the Azores and

71
00:05:48.231 --> 00:05:53.654
how this trip will be just as memorable, but in the end, I release anyone who is hesitant.

72
00:05:54.794 --> 00:05:57.996
In uncharted waters I will need commitment not fear.

73
00:05:59.397 --> 00:06:01.738
I will need collaboration not dissent.

74
00:06:02.538 --> 00:06:05.340
I will need boldness to face whatever we may have to face.

75
00:06:06.461 --> 00:06:11.303
And so my crew is mostly young, with a few adventurous old sailors who will help me guide

76
00:06:11.343 --> 00:06:12.244
us to safe harbors.

77
00:06:13.576 --> 00:06:16.300
We are poised at the beginning of a voyage of discovery.

78
00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.122
What will the outcome be?

79
00:06:19.824 --> 00:06:25.230
Will we come back triumphant with charts of new shores to add to Prince Henry's fame, or will

80
00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:26.432
we never be heard from again?
